
 NZEUS SD Linux - Debian 12 "bookworm" on the R36S console (modern kernel)

 Where things are:
   /            this system, on the SD card (partition "nzroot", 4 GiB).
                It becomes writable only after a file system check at start-up.
   /data        your files (partition "nzdata", ~39 GiB). Kept when the
                system is rebuilt from the PC - put everything you care about here.
   /mnt/stock   the boot files (kernel, device tree), READ-ONLY on purpose.
                Never change that: a wrong file there stops the console starting.
   /tmp         RAM, only 8 MB. No big files there.

 Useful commands:
   nzeus-status            kernel, memory, battery, card, read-only/read-write, USB
   poweroff                switch off cleanly (or hold the power button 3 s)
   nzeus-deck start|stop   NZEUS Deck on the screen (auto-start: touch /data/nzeus/autostart-modern)
   usbnet-up / usbnet-down network over the USB cable (linux-port/usbnet/README.md)
   usb-host-trial          USB HOST mode for a while (UNPLUG THE PC FIRST), then back
                           by itself; usb-host-confirm keeps it, usb-host-off leaves it
   usb-host-status         what is plugged in;  wifi-up / wifi-down  Wi-Fi + SSH
                           (settings: /data/nzeus/wifi.conf, chmod 600; linux-port/usbhost)
   apt update; apt install NAME     more programs (needs a network; RAM is tight:
                                    with ~39 MB, apt is slow - see the README)

 If "/" says READ-ONLY: the start-up check found a problem and did NOT force a
 repair. Switch off and on once; if it stays read-only, hold FN at power-on for
 the RAM rescue and see linux-port/rootfs/PLAN.md ("Recovery").

